      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's political context is crucial - set in Cold War Berlin, with the wall literally dividing the city as marriage divides its characters. This political-personal parallel influenced later works like <a href=\"/movie/582\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"582\">The Lives of Others</a> (2006) and <a href=\"/movie/254578\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"254578\">Phoenix</a> (2014), though neither matches Possession's surreal intensity. Sam Neill and Isabelle Adjani's performances push past melodrama into something primal, establishing a template for extreme art-horror that influenced films like <a href=\"/movie/393519\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"393519\">Raw</a> (2016) and <a href=\"/movie/630240\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"630240\">Titane</a> (2021)."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The creature effects by Carlo Rambaldi (fresh from <a href=\"/movie/348\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"348\">Alien</a> (1979)) represent a high point of practical horror effects, creating something both grotesque and eerily sensual. This combination of psychological drama with body horror would influence decades of filmmakers, from David Cronenberg to Julia Ducournau. The film's unique blend of marital drama and cosmic horror prefigures works like <a href=\"/movie/931\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"931\">Don't Look Now</a> (1973) and Andrzej Żuławski's own <a href=\"/movie/27072\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"27072\">On the Silver Globe</a> (1988). SUBHEAD: The New French Extremity Connection"
      },
